WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.193
What's going to happen

00:00:01.193 --> 00:00:03.883
is robots will be able to do
everything better than us.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.330
I mean, all of us, you know.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.782
I'm not sure exactly
what to do about this.

00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:12.110
Tech billionaire, Elon Musk

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.970
isn't the only one freaking out

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.010
over our new robot overlords.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.650
Fears of automation
induced mass unemployment

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.600
are at the center of a push
for a universal basic income

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.360
or UBI in which the
government sends everyone

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.020
a monthly check, regardless of wealth.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.630
Though the idea has been
kicking around for centuries,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.480
it really caught fire recently
because of Andrew Yang

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.830
who ran for the Democratic
Presidential Nomination in 2020,

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.170
and to be mayor of New York in 2021.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.380
His new memoir and political
party both called "Forward"

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.250
put UBI at the center of his agenda.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.770
Last year, polls showed a
majority favoring the idea,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.490
and cities like Los Angeles,
Saint Paul, Atlanta,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.920
and Newark are experimenting
with or considering local UBIs.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.140
Yang says cash payments are
better than government programs

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.110
for the same reason

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.380
that the libertarian
economist, Milton Friedman,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.545
advocated slashing social welfare

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:06.200
in favor of direct subsidies to the poor

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.400
because it's more effective
to put unrestricted cash,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.830
directly in the hands
of those who need it.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.240
This is one thing that I believe

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.200
I'm fundamentally aligned
with libertarians on.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.810
It's like I think people know best

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.570
how to solve their own
problems and what they need.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.550
Than a government program,

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.220
I think that a lot of these bureaucracies

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.430
are very inefficient.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.570
They're also somewhat punitive

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.373
where I talked to people
who are subject to them

00:01:30.373 --> 00:01:32.240
and they live in fear

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.280
and anxiety all the time

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.210
that they're going to
fill out a form wrong

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.210
or miss an interview,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.860
and then have it taken away from them.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.260
I really like Andrew Yang's disposition,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.800
his refusal to engage in the
dumbest cultural war skirmishes

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.520
and his willingness to call
out Republicans and Democrats.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.890
But I think he's wrong to claim

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.080
that a UBI wouldn't do massive damage

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.470
to American labor markets and
the federal balance sheet.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.560
During the pandemic, the
government shelled out

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.740
for three rounds of stimulus checks

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.790
to about 90% of households,

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.890
including sending money to the well-to-do

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.030
and it supplemented unemployment benefits

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.570
by as much as $600 a week.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.410
One of the negatives of
this time in my mind, Nick,

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.400
is that we're tying so much
of the money to not working.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.010
You know, like I talked to
a 25 year old young person

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.360
who is getting 500-600 bucks a week

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.860
and he said like, "look, I'm
not going to look for a job

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.220
until this trading runs out."

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.290
Yang says that an advantage of a UBI

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.850
is that it's not tied
to employment status.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.010
But I think giving
people money for nothing

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.000
would make them less likely to show up

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.340
for a hard day's work.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.570
The number of job openings
in the United States

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.410
has reached historic highs

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.110
even as the labor force
participation rate,

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.230
the percentage of working age
adults who either have jobs

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.690
or are looking for work

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.590
is almost two points lower
than before the pandemic began.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.860
Here's another problem with the UBI,

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.480
why should middle-class Americans,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.770
not to mention millionaires
and billionaires,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.710
get a check from the government
just for being alive?

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.720
Making it universal has many benefits.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.290
It reduces any stigma,

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.150
it makes it a true right of citizenship,

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.100
it gets rid of all of the
administration and game-playing,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.092
no one's going to be
having to misrepresent

00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:14.600
what their financial circumstances are.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.800
I'm not a big fan of a
lot of the means testings

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.370
because of both the administration

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.720
and the game playing that goes on.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.570
We miss a lot of people every
time we try and draw lines.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.870
Maybe Yang is right that
giving everyone a check

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.430
would make the program
easier to administer.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.760
But does billionaire Elon Musk really need

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.410
even more taxpayer money
than he's already raking in?

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.122
Such largess is
particularly hard to defend

00:03:39.122 --> 00:03:41.940
given that the federal budget
has cracked $6 trillion

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.120
in each of the past two years,

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.030
and the national debt is
approaching $30 trillion.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.480
When he was running for president,

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.510
Yang said the costs of giving
every American citizen,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.740
a monthly check would be
partly covered by phasing out

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.840
certain amounts of existing spending.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.450
Something that virtually never happens.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.640
And even sympathetic
analysis of his plan noted

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:04.900
that its costs would soak up

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.410
all existing federal tax revenues

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.070
resulting in either massive deficits,

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.210
or humongous new tax hikes.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:13.043
Senator Warren,

00:04:13.043 --> 00:04:15.340
I've been talking to
Americans around the country

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.890
about automation and they're smart.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:17.793
They see what's happening around them.

00:04:17.793 --> 00:04:19.780
Their main street stores are closing.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.120
They see a self-serve
kiosk in every McDonald's,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.180
every grocery store, every CVS.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.960
In fact, historically
game-changing innovations

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.360
haven't reduced the total number of jobs.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.890
For all the talk about
the sudden disruptive loss

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.750
of good paying industrial jobs,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.950
the fact is that manufacturing
peaked at around 40%

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.310
of all employment in 1943, since then,

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.600
it's been a steady uninterrupted
decline over decades.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.420
And yet there's been no
shortage of work opportunities

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:47.447
in new industries.

00:04:47.447 --> 00:04:50.190
Driving a truck is the most
common job in 29 states,

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.400
including this one.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.850
Three and a half million
truck drivers in this country,

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.620
and my friends in California

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.590
are piloting self-driving trucks.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.030
Self driving cars,
displacing truck drivers

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.780
and delivery men are likely decades away.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.200
And those productivity gains

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.120
will one day enable entrepreneurs

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.720
to create whole new industries

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.650
with work opportunities not yet conceived.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.880
Even in industries directly
impacted by automation,

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.800
the result is often more
jobs rather than fewer.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.160
There are some structural
issues with our economy,

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.700
where a lot of businesses

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.920
have learned to become
much more efficient,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.460
with a lot fewer workers.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.600
You see it when you go to
a bank and you use an ATM,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:29.970
you don't go to a bank teller.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.880
Obama's analysis is wrong.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.860
After banks rolled at ATM's in the 1990s,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.200
the number of bank tellers
increased in the ensuing decades.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:39.970
And in the years after supermarkets

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.240
and retailers introduced barcode scanning,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.130
the number of cashiers
increased by more than 50%.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.360
Andrew Yang's UBI

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.560
isn't going to be implemented
anywhere anytime soon,

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.480
but enhanced unemployment
benefits are going away.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.580
So we'll soon find out
whether getting less money

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:58.864
from the government spurs more
people to go look for work.

00:05:58.864 --> 00:06:00.030
If and when they do,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.770
job seekers we'll find an economy

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:03.800
where employers are begging for help

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.460
and offering unprecedented
incentives from appetizers,

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.900
to signing bonuses, to free tuition,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.560
to get people to start
punching a clock again.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.340
That's not Andrew Yang's way forward,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.730
but it has the benefit of
reducing dependency on the state

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.680
re-energizing the labor market

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.150
and not making the
government even more broke,

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:23.733
than it already is.

